        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s17">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Shaken Baby Syndrome. Magnetic resonance imaging in a 3-month-old girl with chronic subdural hematoma (SDH). (A) The T1-weighted image shows predominantly low-signal SDH with a high-signal focus in the left frontal region. (B) The T2-weighted image shows predominantly high signal intensity, with a focal area of low intensity. (C) Contrast-enhanced T1-weighted image demonstrates linear dural enhancement. The National Center for Biotechnology Information (MRI by The Korean Radiological Society; http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0)</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="kjr-2-171-g001" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s18">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Shaken Baby Syndrome on Magnetic Resonance Imaging, 8-Month-Old Patient. Chronic subdural hematoma (SDH) in an 8-month-old boy. (A) T1-weighted image shows low-signal SDH in both frontal regions, with a high-signal focus in the right frontal area suggesting subacute hemorrhage (black arrow). (B) T2-weighted image reveals predominantly high-signal SDH with a focal area of low intensity (black arrow). (C) Contrast-enhanced T1-weighted image demonstrates diffuse linear dural enhancement. Contributed by The National Center for Biotechnology Information (MRI by The Korean Radiological Society; http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0)</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="kjr-2-171-g002" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s19">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Grade 1 Retinal Hemorrhage. Few intraretinal hemorrhages are observed in the posterior pole, confined to a limited area around the optic disc and macula. The distribution is sparse and does not extend to the periphery. &#x000b7;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; Adapted from 29149395</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Grade__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s20">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Grade 2 Retinal Hemorrhage. A moderate number of intraretinal hemorrhages are present, involving both the posterior pole and extending toward the midperiphery. Hemorrhages may appear in multiple retinal layers but remain relatively localized. &#x000b7;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; Adapted from 29149395</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Grade__2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s21">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Grade 3a Retinal Hemorrhage. Numerous retinal hemorrhages are seen across multiple layers, diffusely distributed from the posterior pole to the peripheral retina. The pattern suggests significant ocular injury, often associated with abusive head trauma. &#x000b7;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; Adapted from 29149395</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Grade__3a" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28960.s22">
        <fig id="article-28960.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Grade 3b Retinal Hemorrhage. Extensive, confluent retinal hemorrhages are present in all retinal layers, reaching the ora serrata. This severe pattern is highly suggestive of abusive head trauma and indicates substantial acceleration-deceleration forces. &#x000b7;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; Adapted from 29149395</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Grade__3b" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
